{
    "$schema": "http://json-schema.org/draft-04/schema#",
    "id": "urn:OCPP:1.6:2019:12:DataTransferRequest",
    "title": "DataTransferRequest",
    "type": "object",
    "properties": {
        "vendorId": {
            "type": "string",
            "maxLength": 255
        },
        "messageId": {
            "type": "string",
            "maxLength": 50
        },
        "data": {
            "type": "string"
        }
    },
    "additionalProperties": false,
    "required": [
        "vendorId"
    ]
}

Eg. :

"DataTransfer",{"vendorId":"QUENCH","messageId":"VehicleID","data":"e00ee105d1d7"}]



✅ 1. Valid JSON String
"data": "{\"status\":\"OK\",\"uptime\":1200}"

This is a string that contains a JSON structure.

It's how you send structured data when the schema only allows a string.

When received, the system can deserialize this string to access the internal fields (status, uptime).

// Valid - string with JSON content
{
  "vendorId": "VendorX",
  "data": "{\"status\":\"OK\",\"uptime\":1200}"
}

// Invalid - object instead of string
{
  "vendorId": "VendorX",
  "data": {
    "status": "OK",
    "uptime": 1200
  }
}

